// The save-transfer and device-sync Worker (GitHub issue #276). Run it locally with `pnpm dev:sync` (no Cloudflare account needed),
// deploy with `pnpm --filter @mc/sync-worker deploy` (production) or `... deploy --env staging`.
//
// Free-plan budget: 100,000 Worker requests per day, and Durable Objects are free only when SQLite-backed (hence
// `new_sqlite_classes` below), with their own daily caps on requests and on rows read and written. A transfer costs a
// few of each (one upload, one fetch, one alarm), far under the caps for a hobby game. If the Worker is ever abused,
// set TRANSFERS_ENABLED to anything but "true" and redeploy: every /transfer route answers 503 at once.
//
// A flood still spends quota: each request costs one Worker request plus 1-3 Durable Object requests, even when the
// caller is blocked. TRANSFERS_ENABLED is the response to a flood.
//
// Device sync (/sync, one SyncSpace Durable Object per space) costs, in rows written (the free plan allows 100,000 a
// day, and rows read 5 million):
//   - a batch writes 1 row for the space's meta, plus 2 per accepted op that creates a record and 3 per op that rewrites
//     or deletes one (the record's stub, its new version-index row, and the old index row removed); a conflicting op
//     writes nothing. A request that only reads writes 2 rows (meta and alarm) once a day, else none.
//   - every sync request also writes about 2 rows in the caller's RateLimiter object (count, then refund).
//   - reads: a batch reads the meta and one stub per distinct key; a changes page reads the meta and each record it
//     returns.
// A push during a game is one changes pull and one batch of the game's log chunk and meta: 1 + 2 x 3 = 7 rows plus 4 for
// the two requests' limiter rows, 11 rows, 2 Worker requests and 6 Durable Object requests (each sync request is 3:
// limiter twice, space once). The client pushes a game once a round (`sync-scheduler.ts`), and at most 50 times an hour
// however short the rounds are. So an hour of play is about 550 rows at the most (a push that crosses into a new log
// chunk costs 3 rows more than one that doesn't, and one that starts a chunk 1 less), 100 Worker requests and 300 Durable Object
// requests, and the 100,000-row cap is at least 180 player-hours a day, shared with whatever else ran; at a round every
// 3 minutes (20 pushes an hour) it is about 450. One push per command (about 600 an hour) would be 6,600 rows an hour,
// about 15 player-hours a day, with the per-space and per-IP caps below raised to allow it. Past a daily free cap the
// calls fail until 00:00 UTC, and nothing is billed on the Workers Free plan.
//
// Worst case: each IP is held to 5,000 rows of writes per day (the spaces' rows plus the limiter's own, counted as the
// space reports them), so the account's 100,000 rows are spent by about 19 IPs that each use their whole allowance
// (the last request may overshoot by a batch, at most 301 rows); a flood from many addresses is what SYNC_ENABLED is
// for. One IP can also make 3 new spaces a day, 300 requests per 10 minutes, and a space takes 60 batches and 1,500 ops
// an hour. A space holds at most MAX_SPACE_BYTES (10 MiB, counting encoded data and 64 bytes per record row) and is
// deleted after a year unused. Set a TURNSTILE_SECRET to require the human check on creating a space too.
// SYNC_ENABLED is the sync kill switch, as TRANSFERS_ENABLED is the transfer one: anything but "true" makes every /sync
// route answer 503.
//
// Global win rates (/global, one GlobalStats Durable Object named "global"). A result writes, in one put, 1 dedupe row +
// 1 villain + 1 per modular set (at most 6) + 1 per distinct hero (at most 4) + 1 card row + 1 bookkeeping row: 6 for a
// typical solo game, 20 at the worst; a retry writes none. The caller's limiter object writes 2 more per result (its
// state and its alarm), so 22 at the worst. Reads write no limiter row (their allowance is counted in memory) and only one
// summary row an hour in GlobalStats. Per IP: 60 results a day is at most 960 rows, and 120 reads an hour cost none.
// Whole store: 3,000 results a day at the worst is 3,000 x 22 = 66,000 rows of the 100,000. Maintenance is an alarm
// step every 10 minutes while old dedupe rows remain: at most 144 x 102 = 14,700 rows a day, normally a few hundred a
// month. The summary read at 24 rows a day is noise. Worst day in total is about 81,000 rows, leaving the rest for
// sync. Dedupe rows go once two months old. The store only takes games whose villain, modular sets and heroes are on
// the generated allowlist (src/global-allowlist.ts, checked against the client's card pool by a client test), so it
// holds about 1,000 aggregate rows; GLOBAL_MAX_KEYS (5,000) is a backstop real content never reaches. GLOBAL_STATS_ENABLED
// is the kill switch: anything but "true" makes every /global route answer 503.
//
// Replay share links (/replay, one R2 bucket, GitHub issue #693). R2's free tier is 10 GB stored, 1 million Class A
// operations (writes) and 10 million Class B operations (reads) a month, and egress is free. Unlike the caps above, R2
// past its free tier is billed (turning R2 on asks for a payment method), so the Worker caps what it writes:
//   - An upload costs one Worker request, one RateLimiter request (at most 2 rows: its state, and its alarm when the
//     window is new), one ReplayQuota request (1 row) and one R2 write (a second only on an id collision, at 72 random
//     bits never in practice). If the R2 write fails, a second ReplayQuota request (1 row) gives the slot back. An
//     upload refused by either limit costs its Durable Object requests but writes no row and nothing to R2.
//   - Per IP: 30 uploads a day (REPLAY_UPLOADS_PER_IP_PER_DAY). Whole account: MAX_REPLAYS_PER_DAY (200) a UTC day,
//     counted by the one ReplayQuota object; past it an upload answers 503 "full" until midnight UTC. Each upload is
//     at most MAX_REPLAY_BYTES (256 KiB; a typical replay is tens of KB).
//   - Worst case, a flood from many addresses: 200 writes a day, about 6,200 a month, under 1% of the 1 million.
//     Storage grows at most 200 x 256 KiB = 52.4 MB a day; a lifecycle rule deletes each replay 180 days after upload
//     (R2 may take up to a day more), so at most 181 x 52.4 MB = 9.49 GB is held, inside the 10 GB. Keep
//     MAX_REPLAYS_PER_DAY x MAX_REPLAY_BYTES x 181 under 10 GB when changing either var, or this bound breaks.
//   - A view costs one Worker request and one R2 read (Class B), no Durable Object; the response is marked immutable,
//     so a browser that has seen a replay does not ask again. 10 million reads a month is about 330,000 a day, more
//     than the Worker's 100,000 daily requests allow, so reads cannot pass R2's free tier.
//   - Watch R2 usage on the dashboard anyway; REPLAYS_ENABLED set to anything but "true" makes every /replay route
//     answer 503. Create the buckets and their expiry rules once, before the first deploy that carries this binding:
//   wrangler r2 bucket create mc-replays
//   wrangler r2 bucket lifecycle add mc-replays expire-replays --expire-days 180 --force
//   wrangler r2 bucket create mc-replays-staging
//   wrangler r2 bucket lifecycle add mc-replays-staging expire-replays --expire-days 180 --force
//
// Live game rooms (/room, one GameRoom Durable Object per room, GitHub issue #876, docs/live-game-rooms.md). A room is a
// relay: the host's device runs the engine and streams its log in, the room fans it out to watchers over WebSockets
// (Hibernation API). A hibernated room is evicted after seconds of quiet, so the log is stored in frozen chunks:
//   - Creating a room costs one Worker request, one RateLimiter request (at most 2 rows: its state, and its alarm when
//     the window is new) and one GameRoom request that writes 2 rows (meta, and the idle alarm). Per IP: 20 rooms a day
//     (DEFAULT_ROOMS_PER_IP_PER_DAY in room-protocol.ts), so one address writes at most 80 rows a day to open rooms.
//   - A connection is one Worker request and one Durable Object request. Each incoming WebSocket message counts as 1/20
//     of a request; outgoing messages are free. A game of 600 commands an hour sent one per message is about 30 requests
//     an hour, plus each watcher's join and its few messages. A hibernated room costs no duration.
//   - Rows: one header row at the game's start, and one row per completed chunk of 20 commands (written once when the
//     chunk fills, never rewritten; the unfinished tail stays in memory, is written to one t1:tail row each time the
//     host's socket closes or is replaced, when the game ends (with the end), and while the host streams when a batch
//     arrives over 60 seconds after the last write (at most 60 rows an hour, overwritten, deleted when a chunk
//     supersedes it, a rewind cuts it or a log replaces it; a close in a woken room that has not loaded the table
//     writes nothing, so it cannot overwrite the stored tail) and what a wake still lacks is re-asked from the host),
//     so about 1 row per 20 commands plus up to 60 tail rows an hour: a 600-command hour is about 30 chunk rows plus at
//     most 60 tail rows, so up to about 90 rows an hour, and a long 3,000-command game about 150 chunk rows plus a
//     tail row a minute while it streams. A host that leaves after a pause leaves watchers the log to within about a
//     minute of its last command (the tail row is at most a minute old), not necessarily the whole of it. The
//     host's last-message stamp is one meta row at most hourly. The idle alarm is set at creation and moved out only when
//     it fires with the host still active (about once per 12 hours); the end of a game writes 2 rows (meta and the
//     5-minute alarm), once; an Undo that reopens an ended game costs 2 more; a rewind deletes the chunks past its
//     cut; closing deletes everything. Reads: every wake of a room re-reads the header, all
//     its chunks and the tail (ensureLoaded), one read row each, not once per watcher join; a 3,000-command game
//     (150 chunks) woken 300 times costs about 45,000 rows read, so the 5 million a day allowance covers about 100 such
//     games a day. The host is not sent resend on hello; a woken room rewinds its watchers to what it holds before it
//     takes the host's first batch. One room is a few dozen to a few hundred rows; at 100,000 rows a day, that is hundreds of games a
//     day shared with sync and global stats, and a flood from many addresses is what ROOMS_ENABLED is for.
//   - Lobby rooms (POST /room with { "lobby": true }, docs/live-game-rooms.md section 8) add, to the room's own rows:
//     one RoomCode request that writes 2 rows (the code's mapping and its alarm) when the room is made, and 1 extra
//     request per code collision (none written; 31^6 is about 887 million codes). The code's expiry is the room's own: the
//     room moves it with its idle alarm (one RoomCode request, 2 rows, about every 12 hours) and deletes it when it
//     closes (one request, its rows deleted); an alarm there drops a mapping whose room never came back. A code lookup
//     (GET /room/code/:code) is one Worker request, one RateLimiter request and one RoomCode request, and writes no row:
//     the per-IP allowance (60 lookups an hour, ROOM_LOOKUPS_PER_IP_PER_HOUR) is a soft limit: it is counted in memory, so
//     it resets when the limiter object is evicted, and the real defence against guessing is the 31^6 code space. The
//     code's expiry is the room's own plus an hour (ROOM_CODE_MARGIN_MS), so the two alarms never race, and a refresh
//     writes a missing entry back for the same room. The lobby is one `lobby` row (settings, seats, seat-token hashes)
//     in the room, written only when the host sets or changes it, when a seat's holder changes (assigned, released, taken
//     back, or a token restores it) and once when the host's build is first stamped or changes: a few dozen rows for a
//     whole session. Seat requests and their withdrawal are memory only, and a participant's lobby messages are held to
//     a burst of 10 then 2 a second, so no loop of them writes a row. A socket that piles up 50 soft refusals in 10
//     seconds is closed. Never one row per message. Chat writes nothing: the last 50 lines
//     and the rate buckets live in memory, and each chat message costs 1/20 of a Durable Object request like any other.
//     A room that hibernates forgets the chat backlog; its seats, settings and codes survive.
//     Seat decks (`seat-deck`, each player brings their own hero and deck, 2026-11-20) are stored in the same `lobby` row,
//     with their seat: an accepted, changed deck rewrites that one row (the same deck again writes nothing), a change of
//     holder that clears a holder's own deck is already a row write for the holder change, and a `deck-refused` or a
//     `seat-decks-request` writes nothing. A deck is at most 16 KiB (MAX_SEAT_DECK_BYTES) so four decks and the rest of the
//     row stay far under a storage value's 128 KiB. Each participant may send a burst of 4 decks, then one every 3 seconds,
//     and the whole room a burst of 20 then one every 2 seconds, so no loop of decks writes more than about 1,800 rows an
//     hour; a normal lobby writes a handful. The lobby broadcast carries only a summary of each deck (hero, aspects, name,
//     card count, mods), the full lists go to the host alone, on request.
//   - ROOMS_TURNSTILE ("false" by default) makes POST /room require the Turnstile token as well when TURNSTILE_SECRET is
//     set; the secret is shared with the other routes, and the game's room client sends no token yet, so setting the
//     secret alone must not stop rooms.
//   - MAX_ROOM_LOG_BYTES (8 MiB) caps one table's commands; past it the room refuses "too-large" and ends the table
//     for watchers. A joining watcher is sent the log in parts of about 512 KiB, so no message nears the 1 MiB cap.
//   - MAX_ROOM_PARTICIPANTS (25) caps watchers in one room (a valid host token is always let in); MAX_ROOM_MESSAGE_BYTES (64 KiB) caps a message,
//     except a start or log, which may be up to about 1 MiB. A room closes 12 hours after the host's last message and
//     5 minutes after every table has ended. ROOMS_ENABLED set to anything but "true" makes every /room route answer
//     503 (a live room's open sockets stay until they close; deploy with the switch off and they end on their own).
//
// Optional bot check: `wrangler secret put TURNSTILE_SECRET` (add `--env staging` for staging). It is a secret, not a
// var, so it is not listed below; when it is set, a transfer upload, a replay upload and a sync space creation must
// carry a valid CF-Turnstile-Response header.
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "mc-sync",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-10-01",

  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [
      { "name": "TRANSFER_ROOM", "class_name": "TransferRoom" },
      { "name": "RATE_LIMITER", "class_name": "RateLimiter" },
      { "name": "SYNC_SPACE", "class_name": "SyncSpace" },
      { "name": "GLOBAL_STATS", "class_name": "GlobalStats" },
      { "name": "REPLAY_QUOTA", "class_name": "ReplayQuota" },
      { "name": "GAME_ROOM", "class_name": "GameRoom" },
      { "name": "ROOM_CODE", "class_name": "RoomCode" },
    ],
  },
  "migrations": [
    { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["TransferRoom", "RateLimiter"] },
    { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SyncSpace"] },
    { "tag": "v3", "new_sqlite_classes": ["GlobalStats"] },
    { "tag": "v4", "new_sqlite_classes": ["ReplayQuota"] },
    { "tag": "v5", "new_sqlite_classes": ["GameRoom"] },
    { "tag": "v6", "new_sqlite_classes": ["RoomCode"] },
  ],

  "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "REPLAYS", "bucket_name": "mc-replays" }],

  "vars": {
    "TRANSFERS_ENABLED": "true",
    "MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES": "2097152",
    "SYNC_ENABLED": "true",
    "GLOBAL_STATS_ENABLED": "true",
    "MAX_SPACE_BYTES": "10485760",
    "REPLAYS_ENABLED": "true",
    "MAX_REPLAY_BYTES": "262144",
    "MAX_REPLAYS_PER_DAY": "200",
    "ROOMS_ENABLED": "true",
    "MAX_ROOM_PARTICIPANTS": "25",
    "MAX_ROOM_MESSAGE_BYTES": "65536",
    "MAX_ROOM_LOG_BYTES": "8388608",
    "ROOMS_TURNSTILE": "false",
    // Comma list; a `*` in the host matches a name (or, after a colon, a port). Only these origins get CORS headers.
    // Netlify previews and aliases, local dev servers, and the Capacitor and Tauri webviews.
    "ALLOWED_ORIGINS": "https://marvel.champions-digital-game.com,https://marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,https://*--marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,http://localhost:*,http://127.0.0.1:*,https://localhost,capacitor://localhost,tauri://localhost,http://tauri.localhost,https://tauri.localhost",
  },

  // Wrangler environments do not inherit bindings or vars, so staging repeats them.
  "env": {
    "staging": {
      "name": "mc-sync-staging",
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [
          { "name": "TRANSFER_ROOM", "class_name": "TransferRoom" },
          { "name": "RATE_LIMITER", "class_name": "RateLimiter" },
          { "name": "SYNC_SPACE", "class_name": "SyncSpace" },
          { "name": "GLOBAL_STATS", "class_name": "GlobalStats" },
          { "name": "REPLAY_QUOTA", "class_name": "ReplayQuota" },
          { "name": "GAME_ROOM", "class_name": "GameRoom" },
          { "name": "ROOM_CODE", "class_name": "RoomCode" },
        ],
      },
      "migrations": [
        { "tag": "v1", "new_sqlite_classes": ["TransferRoom", "RateLimiter"] },
        { "tag": "v2", "new_sqlite_classes": ["SyncSpace"] },
        { "tag": "v3", "new_sqlite_classes": ["GlobalStats"] },
        { "tag": "v4", "new_sqlite_classes": ["ReplayQuota"] },
        { "tag": "v5", "new_sqlite_classes": ["GameRoom"] },
        { "tag": "v6", "new_sqlite_classes": ["RoomCode"] },
      ],
      "r2_buckets": [{ "binding": "REPLAYS", "bucket_name": "mc-replays-staging" }],
      "vars": {
        "TRANSFERS_ENABLED": "true",
        "MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES": "2097152",
        "SYNC_ENABLED": "true",
        "GLOBAL_STATS_ENABLED": "true",
        "MAX_SPACE_BYTES": "10485760",
        "REPLAYS_ENABLED": "true",
        "MAX_REPLAY_BYTES": "262144",
        "MAX_REPLAYS_PER_DAY": "200",
        "ROOMS_ENABLED": "true",
        "MAX_ROOM_PARTICIPANTS": "25",
        "MAX_ROOM_MESSAGE_BYTES": "65536",
        "MAX_ROOM_LOG_BYTES": "8388608",
        "ROOMS_TURNSTILE": "false",
        "ALLOWED_ORIGINS": "https://marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,https://*--marvel-champions-digital-game.netlify.app,http://localhost:*,http://127.0.0.1:*,https://localhost,capacitor://localhost,tauri://localhost,http://tauri.localhost,https://tauri.localhost",
      },
    },
  },
}
